{
  "id": "u03-l05",
  "unit": "u03-family",
  "title": "I have…",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali has no verb 'to have'. Instead you say **'to me there is'**: **আমার একটা বোন আছে** (*amar ekta bon achhe*) — literally 'My one sister exists' = 'I have a sister.' Note it's **আমার**, not আমি. The negative uses **নেই**: **আমার ভাই নেই** = 'I have no brother.' Question: **তোমার কি ভাই আছে?**\n\nWhen you count, Bengali adds a **classifier** to the number. For people use **-জন** (*-jon*): **একজন** (one person), **দুইজন** (two people), **কয়জন?** (how many people?). In casual speech **-টা** is common too: **একটা বোন**.\n\nPlural possessives: আমরা → **আমাদের** (our), তোমরা → **তোমাদের** (your, to two or more people you'd call তুমি). Others follow the same pattern: *apnader*, *oder*, *tader*.\n\nCulture: 'How many brothers and sisters do you have?' is a perfectly normal first-meeting question in Bangladesh — as are questions about marriage and children. It's friendly interest, not nosiness.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "ekta",
      "bn": "একটা",
      "tr": "ekta",
      "en": "one; a",
      "notes": "এক + classifier -টা. The everyday 'a / one (thing)'. Formal/written: একটি."
    },
